The following example illustrates the caveat below.



+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i19 : D = digraph {{b,{a}},{a,{c}},{c,{}}}                   |
|                                                             |
|o19 = Digraph{a => {c}}                                      |
|              b => {a}                                       |
|              c => {}                                        |
|                                                             |
|o19 : Digraph                                                |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i20 : R = markovRing (2,3,2)                                 |
|                                                             |
|o20 = R                                                      |
|                                                             |
|o20 : PolynomialRing                                         |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i21 : VarNames = {b,a,c}                                     |
|                                                             |
|o21 = {b, a, c}                                              |
|                                                             |
|o21 : List                                                   |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i22 : S = globalMarkov D                                     |
|                                                             |
|o22 = {{{b}, {c}, {a}}}                                      |
|                                                             |
|o22 : List                                                   |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i23 : conditionalIndependenceIdeal(R, S, VarNames) / print;  |
|- p     p      + p     p                                     |
|   1,1,2 2,1,1    1,1,1 2,1,2                                |
|- p     p      + p     p                                     |
|   1,2,2 2,2,1    1,2,1 2,2,2                                |
|- p     p      + p     p                                     |
|   1,3,2 2,3,1    1,3,1 2,3,2                                |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i24 : vertices D                                             |
|                                                             |
|o24 = {c, a, b}                                              |
|                                                             |
|o24 : List                                                   |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i25 : conditionalIndependenceIdeal(R, S, vertices D) / print;|
|- p     p      + p     p                                     |
|   1,1,2 2,1,1    1,1,1 2,1,2                                |
|- p     p      + p     p                                     |
|   1,2,2 2,2,1    1,2,1 2,2,2                                |
|- p     p      + p     p                                     |
|   1,3,2 2,3,1    1,3,1 2,3,2                                |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

We note that the list of random variable names must be in the same order as the
implicit order used in the sequence $d$. In the previous example, we have the
graph $b \to a \to c$, where $a$ has three states and $b$ and $c$ are both
binary. Note that the ring $R$ was created with the sequence $d = (2,3,2)$,
having in mind the topological order of the graph as opposed to the vertex
labels. Note how the first instance of this method returns the correct output,
however, the second instance returns an incorrect ideal since vertices D is not
in the same order as the sequence $d$.



